    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d806">

                <pb facs="1065" n="1053" sameAs="frus:frus1945v05/pg1053"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.94114 Supplies/1–1945</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The British First Secretary of Embassy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gore-Booth</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Assistant Chief of the Special War Problems
                        Division</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Kuppinger</hi>)<anchor n="33" xml:id="d806-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">120/14/45</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-01-19">January 19,
                        1945</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. Kuppinger</hi>: With further reference to
                    your letter of the 4th January<anchor n="34" xml:id="d806-f2"/> regarding the
                    continued use of a Soviet Pacific port for the trans-shipment of relief supplies
                    for Allied nationals in <pb facs="1066" n="1054" xml:id="pg1054"/> Japanese
                    custody, I am sending you herewith the text of a telegram which the Foreign
                    Office have addressed to His Majesty’s Ambassador in Moscow,<anchor n="35"
                        xml:id="d806-f3"/> from which you will see that His Majesty’s Ambassador has
                    been instructed to acquaint the Soviet Government of the concern of the United
                    Kingdom and British Dominions in the representations which the United States
                    Ambassador at Moscow has made to the Soviet Government looking towards their
                    consent to further shipments being made through the port of Nakhodka.</p>
                <p>Yours sincerely,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paul Gore-Booth</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d806-f1" n="33" type="source" xml:id="d806-f1-content">
                <p>Eldred D. Kuppinger, in acknowledging the receipt of this letter on January 26,
                    included the substance of telegrams 173, January 18, 8 p.m., from Moscow, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">supra;</hi> 128, January 20, 7 p.m., to Moscow, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">infra;</hi> and 181, January 27, 8 p.m., to Moscow, <ref
                        target="#pg1054">p. 1054</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d806-f2" n="34" xml:id="d806-f2-content">
                <p>See <ref target="frus1944v04#pg_1197">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1944, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">iv</hi>, p. 1197</ref>, footnote 23.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d806-f3" n="35" xml:id="d806-f3-content">
                <p>Enclosure not printed; Sir Archibald J. K. Clark Kerr was the British Ambassador
                    in the Soviet Union.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
